sn42.8:11.1 | S: They give up killing living creatures. | T: “Having abandoned the taking of life, he refrains from taking life.
sn42.8:11.2 | S: They give up stealing. | T: Having abandoned stealing, he refrains from stealing.
sn42.8:11.3 | S: They give up sexual misconduct. | T: Having abandoned illicit sex, he refrains from illicit sex.
sn42.8:11.4 | S: They give up lying. | T: Having abandoned lies, he refrains from lies.
sn42.8:11.5 | S: They give up backbiting. | T: Having abandoned divisive speech, he refrains from divisive speech.
sn42.8:11.6 | S: They give up harsh speech. | T: Having abandoned harsh speech, he refrains from harsh speech.
sn42.8:11.7 | S: They give up talking nonsense. | T: Having abandoned idle chatter, he refrains from idle chatter.
sn42.8:11.8 | S: They give up covetousness. | T: Having abandoned covetousness, he becomes uncovetous.
sn42.8:11.9 | S: They give up ill will and malevolence. | T: Having abandoned ill will & anger, he becomes one with a mind of no ill will.
sn42.8:11.10 | S: They give up wrong view and have right view. | T: Having abandoned wrong views, he becomes one who has right views.
sn42.8:12.1 | S: That noble disciple is rid of desire, rid of ill will, unconfused, aware, and mindful. They meditate spreading a heart full of love to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, they spread a heart full of love to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: “That disciple of the noble ones, headman—thus devoid of covetousness, devoid of ill will, unbewildered, alert, mindful—keeps pervading the first direction [the east] with an awareness imbued with goodwill, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, he keeps pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with goodwill—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.
sn42.8:12.2 | S: Suppose there was a powerful horn blower. They’d easily make themselves heard in the four quarters. | T: Just as a strong conch-trumpet blower can notify the four directions without any difficulty,
