## an5.34
an5.34:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Vesālī, at the Great Wood, in the hall with the peaked roof. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Vesālī at the Gabled Hall in the Great Forest.
an5.34:1.2 | S: Then General Sīha went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and asked him, | T: Then General Sīha went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an5.34:1.3 | S: “Sir, can you point out a fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life?” | T: “Is it possible, lord, to point out a fruit of giving visible in the here & now?”
an5.34:2.1 | S: “I can, Sīha,” said the Buddha. | T: “It is possible, Sīha.
an5.34:2.2 | S: “A giver, a donor is dear and beloved to many people. | T: One who is generous, a master of giving, is dear & charming to people at large.
an5.34:2.3 | S: This is a fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: And the fact that who is generous, a master of giving, is dear & charming to people at large: This is a fruit of giving visible in the here & now.
an5.34:3.1 | S: Furthermore, good and true persons associate with a giver. | T: “And further, good people, people of integrity, admire one who is generous, a master of giving.
an5.34:3.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: And the fact that good people, people of integrity, admire one who is generous, a master of giving: This, too, is a fruit of giving visible in the here & now.
an5.34:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a giver gains a good reputation. | T: “And further, the fine reputation of one who is generous, a master of giving, is spread far & wide.
an5.34:4.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: And the fact that the fine reputation of one who is generous, a master of giving, is spread far & wide: This, too, is a fruit of giving visible in the here & now.
an5.34:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a giver enters any kind of assembly bold and self-assured, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: “And further, when one who is generous, a master of giving, approaches any assembly of people—noble warriors, brahmans, householders, or contemplatives—he/she does so confidently & without embarrassment.
an5.34:5.2 | S: This is another fruit of giving that’s apparent in the present life. | T: And the fact that when one who is generous, a master of giving, approaches any assembly of people—noble warriors, brahmans, householders, or contemplatives—he/she does so confidently & without embarrassment: This, too, is a fruit of giving visible in the here & now.
an5.34:6.1 | S: Furthermore, when a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: “And further, at the break-up of the body, after death, one who is generous, a master of giving, reappears in a good destination, a heavenly world.
an5.34:6.2 | S: This is a fruit of giving to do with lives to come.” | T: And the fact that at the break-up of the body, after death, one who is generous, a master of giving, reappears in a good destination, a heavenly world: This is a fruit of giving in the next life.”
an5.34:7.1 | S: When he said this, General Sīha said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, General Sīha said to the Blessed One:
an5.34:7.2 | S: “When it comes to those four fruits of giving that are apparent in the present life, I don’t rely on faith in the Buddha, for I know them too. | T: “As for the four fruits of giving visible in the here & now that have been pointed out by the Blessed One, it’s not the case that I go by conviction in the Blessed One with regard to them. I know them, too.
an5.34:7.3 | S: I’m a giver, a donor, and am dear and beloved to many people. | T: I am generous, a master of giving, dear & charming to people at large.
an5.34:7.4 | S: I’m a giver, and good and true persons associate with me. | T: I am generous, a master of giving; good people, people of integrity, admire me.
an5.34:7.5 | S: I’m a giver, and I have this good reputation: ‘General Sīha gives, serves, and attends on the Saṅgha.’ | T: I am generous, a master of giving, and my fine reputation is spread far & wide: ‘Sīha is generous, a doer, a supporter of the Saṅgha.’
an5.34:7.6 | S: I’m a giver, and I enter any kind of assembly bold and self-assured, whether it’s an assembly of aristocrats, brahmins, householders, or ascetics. | T: I am generous, a master of giving, and when I approach any assembly of people—noble warriors, brahmans, householders, or contemplatives—I do so confidently & without embarrassment.
an5.34:7.7 | S: When it comes to these four fruits of giving that are apparent in the present life, I don’t rely on faith in the Buddha, for I know them too. | T: (none)
an5.34:7.8 | S: But when the Buddha says: | T: “But when the Blessed One says to me,
an5.34:7.9 | S: ‘When a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm.’ I don’t know this, so I have to rely on faith in the Buddha.” | T: ‘At the break-up of the body, after death, one who is generous, a master of giving, reappears in a good destination, a heavenly world,’ that I do not know. That is where I go by conviction in the Blessed One.”
an5.34:7.10 | S: “That’s so true, Sīha! That’s so true! | T: “So it is, Sīha. So it is.
an5.34:7.11 | S: When a giver’s body breaks up, after death, they’re reborn in a good place, a heavenly realm. | T: At the break-up of the body, after death, one who is generous, a master of giving, reappears in a good destination, a heavenly world.”
an5.34:8.1 | S: Giving, you’re loved and befriended by many people. | T: One who gives is dear. People at large admire him.
an5.34:8.2 | S: You get a good reputation, and your fame grows. | T: He gains honor. His status grows.
an5.34:8.3 | S: A generous man enters an assembly | T: He enters an assembly unembarrassed.
an5.34:8.4 | S: bold and self-assured. | T: He is confident—the unmiserly man.
an5.34:9.1 | S: So an astute person, seeking happiness, <j>would give gifts, | T: Therefore the wise give gifts. Seeking bliss,
an5.34:9.2 | S: having driven out the stain of stinginess. | T: they would subdue the stain of miserliness.
an5.34:9.3 | S: They live long in the Third Heaven, | T: Established in the three-fold heavenly world, they enjoy themselves long
an5.34:9.4 | S: enjoying the company of the gods. | T: in fellowship with the devas.
an5.34:10.1 | S: Having taken the opportunity to do good, <j>when they pass from here | T: Having made the opportunity for themselves, having done what is skillful, then when they fall from here
an5.34:10.2 | S: they wander self-radiant in the Garden of Delight. | T: they fare on, self-radiant, in Nandana [the garden of the devas].
an5.34:10.3 | S: There they delight, rejoice, and enjoy themselves, | T: There they delight, enjoy, are joyful,
an5.34:10.4 | S: provided with the five kinds of sensual stimulation. | T: replete with the five sensuality strands.
an5.34:10.5 | S: Having practiced the word of the unattached, <j>the unaffected, | T: Having followed the words of the sage who is Such,
an5.34:10.6 | S: disciples of the Holy One rejoice in heaven.” | T: they enjoy themselves in heaven— disciples of the One Well-Gone.
an5.34:10.7 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.74
an7.74:1.1 | S: “Once upon a time, mendicants, there was a teacher named Araka. He was a religious founder and was free of desire for sensual pleasures. | T: “Once, monks, there was a teacher named Araka, a sectarian leader who was free of passion for sensuality.
an7.74:1.2 | S: He had many hundreds of disciples, | T: He had many hundreds of students and he taught them the Dhamma in this way:
an7.74:1.3 | S: and he taught them like this: | T: (none)
an7.74:1.4 | S: ‘Brahmins, life as a human is short, brief, and fleeting, full of suffering and distress. Be thoughtful and wake up! Do what’s good and lead the spiritual life, for no one born can escape death. | T: ‘Next to nothing, brahmans, is the life of human beings—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:2.1 | S: It’s like a drop of dew on a grass tip. When the sun comes up it vanishes right away and doesn’t last long. | T: “‘Just as a dewdrop on the tip of a blade of grass quickly vanishes with the rising of the sun and does not stay long,
an7.74:2.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a dewdrop. It’s brief and fleeting, full of suffering and distress. Be thoughtful and wake up! Do what’s good and lead the spiritual life, for no one born can escape death. | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a dewdrop—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:3.1 | S: It’s like when the heavens rain heavily. The bubbles vanish right away and don’t last long. | T: “‘Just as when the rain-devas send rain in fat drops, and a bubble on the water quickly vanishes and does not stay long,
an7.74:3.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a bubble. … | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a water bubble—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:4.1 | S: It’s like a line drawn in water. It vanishes right away and doesn’t last long. | T: “‘Just as a line drawn in the water with a stick quickly vanishes and does not stay long,
an7.74:4.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a line drawn in water. … | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a line drawn in the water with a stick—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life.
an7.74:4.3 | P: natthi jātassa amaraṇaṁ. | T: For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:5.1 | S: It’s like a mountain river traveling far, flowing fast, carrying all before it. It doesn’t turn back—not for a moment, a second, an hour—but runs, rolls, and flows on. | T: “‘Just as a river flowing down from the mountains, going far, its current swift, carrying everything with it, so that there is not a moment, an instant, a second where it stands still, but instead it goes & rushes & flows,
an7.74:5.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a mountain river. … | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a river flowing down from the mountains—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life.
an7.74:5.3 | P: natthi jātassa amaraṇaṁ. | T: For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:6.1 | S: It’s like a strong man who has formed a glob of spit on the tip of his tongue. He could easily spit it out. | T: “‘Just as a strong man forming a drop of spit on the tip of his tongue would spit it out with little effort,
an7.74:6.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a glob of spit. … | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a drop of spit—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life.
an7.74:6.3 | P: natthi jātassa amaraṇaṁ. | T: For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:7.1 | S: Suppose there was an iron cauldron that had been heated all day. If you tossed a scrap of meat in, it would vanish right away and not last long. | T: “‘Just as a sliver of meat thrown into an iron pan heated all day quickly vanishes and does not stay long,
an7.74:7.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a scrap of meat. … | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a sliver of meat—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life.
an7.74:7.3 | P: natthi jātassa amaraṇaṁ. | T: For one who is born there is no freedom from death.
an7.74:8.1 | S: It’s like a cow being led to the slaughterhouse. With every step she comes closer to the slaughter, closer to death. | T: “‘Just as a cow to be slaughtered being led to the slaughterhouse, with every step of its foot closer to its slaughtering, closer to death,
an7.74:8.2 | S: In the same way, life as a human is like a cow being slaughtered. It’s brief and fleeting, full of suffering and distress. Be thoughtful and wake up! Do what’s good and lead the spiritual life, for no one born can escape death.’ | T: in the same way, brahmans, the life of human beings is like a cow to be slaughtered—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.’
an7.74:9.1 | S: Now, mendicants, at that time human beings had a lifespan of 60,000 years. Girls could be married at 500 years of age. | T: “Now at that time, monks, the human life span was 60,000 years, with girls marriageable at 500.
an7.74:9.2 | S: And human beings only had six afflictions: | T: And at that time there were (only) six afflictions:
an7.74:9.3 | S: cold, heat, hunger, thirst, and the need to defecate and urinate. | T: cold, heat, hunger, thirst, defecation, & urination.
an7.74:9.4 | S: But even though humans were so long-lived with so few afflictions, Araka still taught in this way: | T: Yet even though people were so long-lived, long-lasting, with so few afflictions, that teacher Araka taught the Dhamma to his disciples in this way:
an7.74:9.5 | S: ‘Life as a human is short, brief, and fleeting, full of suffering and distress. Be thoughtful and wake up! Do what’s good and lead the spiritual life, for no one born can escape death.’ | T: ‘Next to nothing, brahmans, is the life of human beings—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.’
an7.74:10.1 | S: These days it’d be right to say: | T: “At present, monks, one speaking rightly would say,
an7.74:10.2 | S: ‘Life as a human is short, brief, and fleeting, full of suffering and distress. Be thoughtful and wake up! Do what’s good and lead the spiritual life, for no one born can escape death.’ | T: ‘Next to nothing is the life of human beings—limited, trifling, of much stress & many despairs. One should touch this (truth) like a sage, do what is skillful, follow the holy life. For one who is born there is no freedom from death.’
an7.74:10.3 | S: For these days a long life is a hundred years or a little more. | T: At present, monks, one who lives a long time is 100 years old or a little bit more.
an7.74:10.4 | S: Living for a hundred years, there are just three hundred seasons, | T: Living 100 years, one lives for 300 seasons:
an7.74:10.5 | S: a hundred each of the winter, summer, and rains. | T: 100 seasons of cold, 100 seasons of heat, 100 seasons of rain.
an7.74:10.6 | S: Living for three hundred seasons, there are just twelve hundred months, | T: Living for 300 seasons, one lives for 1,200 months:
an7.74:10.7 | S: four hundred in each of the winter, summer, and rains. | T: 400 months of cold, 400 months of heat, 400 months of rain.
an7.74:10.8 | S: Living for twelve hundred months, there are just twenty-four hundred fortnights, | T: Living for 1,200 months, one lives for 2,400 fortnights:
an7.74:10.9 | S: eight hundred in each of the winter, summer, and rains. | T: 800 fortnights of cold, 800 fortnights of heat, 800 fortnights of rain.
an7.74:10.10 | S: Living for 2,400 fortnights, there are just 36,000 days, | T: Living for 2,400 fortnights, one lives for 36,000 days:
an7.74:10.11 | S: 12,000 in each of the summer, winter, and rains. | T: 12,000 days of cold, 12,000 days of heat, 12,000 days of rain.
an7.74:10.12 | S: Living for 36,000 days, you just eat 72,000 meals, | T: Living for 36,000 days, one eats 72,000 meals:
an7.74:10.13 | S: 24,000 in each of the summer, winter, and rains, including when you’re suckling at the breast, and when you’re prevented from eating. | T: 24,000 meals in the cold, 24,000 meals in the heat, 24,000 meals in the rain—counting the taking of mother’s milk and obstacles to eating.
an7.74:11.1 | S: Things that prevent you from eating include anger, pain, sickness, sabbath, or being unable to get food. | T: These are the obstacles to eating: when one doesn’t eat while angered, when one doesn’t eat while suffering or stressed, when one doesn’t eat while sick, when one doesn’t eat on the observance [uposatha] day, when one doesn’t eat while poor.
an7.74:11.2 | S: So mendicants, for a human being with a hundred years lifespan I have counted the lifespan, the limit of the lifespan, the seasons, the years, the months, the fortnights, the nights, the days, the meals, and the things that prevent them from eating. | T: “Thus, monks, I have reckoned the life of a person living for 100 years: I have reckoned the life span, reckoned the seasons, reckoned the years, reckoned the months, reckoned the fortnights, reckoned the nights, reckoned the days, reckoned the meals, reckoned the obstacles to eating.
an7.74:11.3 | S: Out of sympathy, I’ve done what a teacher should do who wants what’s best for their disciples. | T: Whatever a teacher should do—seeking the welfare of his disciples, out of sympathy for them—that have I done for you.
an7.74:11.4 | S: Here are these roots of trees, and here are these empty huts. Practice absorption, mendicants! Don’t be negligent! Don’t regret it later! This is my instruction to you.” | T: Over there are the roots of trees; over there, empty dwellings. Practice jhāna, monks. Don’t be heedless. Don’t later fall into regret. This is our message to you all.”
an7.74:11.5 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)
an7.74:11.6 | P: Mahāvaggo sattamo. | T: (none)
an7.74:12.0 | P: Tassuddānaṁ | T: (none)
an7.74:12.1 | P: Hirīsūriyaṁ upamā, | T: (none)
an7.74:12.2 | P: dhammaññū pārichattakaṁ; | T: (none)
an7.74:12.3 | P: Sakkaccaṁ bhāvanā aggi, | T: (none)
an7.74:12.4 | P: sunettaarakena cāti. | T: (none)

## snp1.8
snp1.8:1.1 | S: Those who are skilled in the meaning of scripture | T: This is to be done by one skilled in aims
snp1.8:1.2 | S: should practice as follows <j>to realize the state of peace. | T: appreciating the state of peace:
snp1.8:1.3 | S: Let them be capable and upright, very upright, | T: Be capable, upright, & straightforward,
snp1.8:1.4 | S: easy to speak to, gentle and humble; | T: easy to instruct, gentle, & not conceited,
snp1.8:2.1 | S: content and unburdensome, | T: content & easy to support,
snp1.8:2.2 | S: unbusied, living lightly, | T: with few duties, living lightly,
snp1.8:2.3 | S: alert, with senses calmed, | T: with peaceful faculties, astute,
snp1.8:2.4 | S: courteous, not fawning on families. | T: modest, & no greed for supporters.
snp1.8:3.1 | S: Let them not do the slightest thing | T: Do not do the slightest thing
snp1.8:3.2 | S: that others who are wise would blame. | T: that the observant would later censure.
snp1.8:3.3 | S: May they be happy and safe! | T: Think: Happy, at rest,
snp1.8:3.4 | S: May all beings be happy! | T: may all beings be happy at heart.
snp1.8:4.1 | S: Whatever living creatures there are | T: Whatever beings there may be,
snp1.8:4.2 | S: with not a one left out— | T: weak or strong, without exception,
snp1.8:4.3 | S: frail or firm, long or large, | T: long, large,
snp1.8:4.4 | S: medium, small, tiny or round, | T: middling, short, subtle, gross,
snp1.8:5.1 | S: seen or unseen, | T: seen & unseen,
snp1.8:5.2 | S: living far or near, | T: living near & far away,
snp1.8:5.3 | S: those who have been born <j>and those about to be born— | T: born or seeking birth:
snp1.8:5.4 | S: may all beings be happy! | T: May all beings be happy at heart.
snp1.8:6.1 | S: Let none deceive another, | T: Let no one deceive another
snp1.8:6.2 | S: nor look down on anyone anywhere. | T: or despise anyone anywhere,
snp1.8:6.3 | S: Though provoked or aggrieved, | T: or, through anger or resistance-perception,
snp1.8:6.4 | S: let them not wish pain on each other. | T: wish for another to suffer.
snp1.8:7.1 | S: Even as a mother would protect with her life | T: As a mother would risk her life
snp1.8:7.2 | S: her child, her only child, | T: to protect her child, her only child,
snp1.8:7.3 | S: so too for all creatures | T: even so should one cultivate the heart limitlessly with regard to all beings.
snp1.8:7.4 | S: unfold a boundless heart. | T: (none)
snp1.8:8.1 | S: With love for the whole world, | T: With goodwill for the entire cosmos,
snp1.8:8.2 | S: unfold a boundless heart: | T: cultivate the heart limitlessly:
snp1.8:8.3 | S: above, below, all round, | T: above, below, & all around,
snp1.8:8.4 | S: unconstricted, without enmity or foe. | T: unobstructed, without hostility or hate.
snp1.8:9.1 | S: When standing, walking, sitting, | T: Whether standing, walking, sitting,

