## sn42.8, continued
sn42.8:9.3 | P: Yaṁ kho pana mayā kāmesu micchā ciṇṇaṁ yāvatakaṁ vā tāvatakaṁ vā taṁ na suṭṭhu, taṁ na sādhu. | T: (none)
sn42.8:9.4 | P: Ahañceva kho pana tappaccayā vippaṭisārī assaṁ, na metaṁ pāpaṁ kammaṁ akataṁ bhavissatī’ti. | T: (none)
sn42.8:9.5 | P: So iti paṭisaṅkhāya tañceva kāmesumicchācāraṁ pajahati, āyatiñca kāmesumicchācārā paṭivirato hoti. | T: (none)
sn42.8:9.6 | P: Evametassa pāpassa kammassa pahānaṁ hoti. | T: (none)
sn42.8:9.7 | P: Evametassa pāpassa kammassa samatikkamo hoti. | T: (none)
sn42.8:10.1 | S: ‘In many ways the Buddha criticizes and denounces lying, saying: “Stop lying!” | T: the telling of lies, and says, “Abstain from the telling of lies.”
sn42.8:10.2 | S: But I have lied to a certain extent. | T: There are lies that I have told, to a greater or lesser extent.
sn42.8:10.3 | S: That’s not right, it’s not good, and I feel remorseful because of it. But I can’t undo what I have done.’ | T: That was not right. That was not good. But if I become remorseful for that reason, that evil deed of mine will not be undone.’
sn42.8:10.4 | S: Reflecting like this, they give up lying, and in future they refrain from lying. | T: So, reflecting thus, he abandons right then the telling of lies, and in the future refrains from telling lies.
sn42.8:10.5 | S: That’s how to give up this bad deed and get past it. | T: This is how there comes to be the abandoning of that evil deed. This is how there comes to be the transcending of that evil deed.
sn42.8:11.1 | S: They give up killing living creatures. | T: “Having abandoned the taking of life, he refrains from taking life.
sn42.8:11.2 | S: They give up stealing. | T: Having abandoned stealing, he refrains from stealing.
sn42.8:11.3 | S: They give up sexual misconduct. | T: Having abandoned illicit sex, he refrains from illicit sex.
sn42.8:11.4 | S: They give up lying. | T: Having abandoned lies, he refrains from lies.
sn42.8:11.5 | S: They give up backbiting. | T: Having abandoned divisive speech, he refrains from divisive speech.
sn42.8:11.6 | S: They give up harsh speech. | T: Having abandoned harsh speech, he refrains from harsh speech.
sn42.8:11.7 | S: They give up talking nonsense. | T: Having abandoned idle chatter, he refrains from idle chatter.
sn42.8:11.8 | S: They give up covetousness. | T: Having abandoned covetousness, he becomes uncovetous.
sn42.8:11.9 | S: They give up ill will and malevolence. | T: Having abandoned ill will & anger, he becomes one with a mind of no ill will.
sn42.8:11.10 | S: They give up wrong view and have right view. | T: Having abandoned wrong views, he becomes one who has right views.
sn42.8:12.1 | S: That noble disciple is rid of desire, rid of ill will, unconfused, aware, and mindful. They meditate spreading a heart full of love to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, they spread a heart full of love to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: “That disciple of the noble ones, headman—thus devoid of covetousness, devoid of ill will, unbewildered, alert, mindful—keeps pervading the first direction [the east] with an awareness imbued with goodwill, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, he keeps pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with goodwill—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.
sn42.8:12.2 | S: Suppose there was a powerful horn blower. They’d easily make themselves heard in the four quarters. | T: Just as a strong conch-trumpet blower can notify the four directions without any difficulty,
sn42.8:12.3 | S: In the same way, when the heart’s release by love has been developed and cultivated like this, any limited deeds they’ve done don’t remain or persist there. | T: in the same way, when the awareness-release through goodwill is thus developed, thus pursued, any deed done to a limited extent no longer remains there, no longer stays there.
sn42.8:13.1 | S: Then that noble disciple is rid of desire, rid of ill will, unconfused, aware, and mindful. They meditate spreading a heart full of compassion … | T: “That disciple of the noble ones—thus devoid of covetousness, devoid of ill will, unbewildered, alert, mindful—keeps pervading the first direction with an awareness imbued with compassion…
sn42.8:13.2 | S: They meditate spreading a heart full of rejoicing … | T: empathetic joy…
sn42.8:13.3 | S: They meditate spreading a heart full of equanimity to one direction, and to the second, and to the third, and to the fourth. In the same way above, below, across, everywhere, all around, they spread a heart full of equanimity to the whole world—abundant, expansive, limitless, free of enmity and ill will. | T: equanimity, likewise the second, likewise the third, likewise the fourth. Thus above, below, & all around, everywhere, in its entirety, he keeps pervading the all-encompassing cosmos with an awareness imbued with equanimity—abundant, enlarged, immeasurable, without hostility, without ill will.
sn42.8:13.4 | S: Suppose there was a powerful horn blower. They’d easily make themselves heard in the four quarters. | T: Just as a strong conch-trumpet blower can notify the four directions without any difficulty,
sn42.8:13.5 | S: In the same way, when the heart’s release by equanimity has been developed and cultivated like this, any limited deeds they’ve done don’t remain or persist there.” | T: in the same way, when the awareness-release through equanimity is thus developed, thus pursued, any deed done to a limited extent no longer remains there, no longer stays there.”
sn42.8:14.1 | S: When he said this, Asibandhaka’s son the chief said to the Buddha, | T: When this was said, Asibandhakaputta the headman, the disciple of the Nigaṇṭhas, said to the Blessed One:
sn42.8:14.2 | S: “Excellent, sir! Excellent! … | T: “Magnificent, lord! Magnificent! Just as if he were to place upright what was overturned, to reveal what was hidden, to show the way to one who was lost, or to carry a lamp into the dark so that those with eyes could see forms, in the same way has the Blessed One—through many lines of reasoning—made the Dhamma clear.
sn42.8:14.3 | S: From this day forth, may the Buddha remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: I go to the Blessed One for refuge, to the Dhamma, & to the Saṅgha of monks. May the Blessed One remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge from this day forward, for life.”
sn42.8:14.4 | P: Aṭṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an7.52
an7.52:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Campā on the banks of the Gaggarā Lotus Pond. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Campā on the shore of Gaggarā Lake.
an7.52:1.2 | S: Then several lay followers of Campā went to Venerable Sāriputta, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then a large number of lay followers from Campā went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there they said to Ven. Sāriputta:
an7.52:1.3 | S: “Sir, it’s been a long time since we’ve heard a Dhamma talk from the Buddha. | T: “It has been a long time, venerable sir, since we have had a chance to hear a Dhamma talk in the Blessed One’s presence.
an7.52:1.4 | S: It would be good if we got to hear a Dhamma talk from the Buddha.” | T: It would be good if we could get to hear a Dhamma talk in the Blessed One’s presence.”
an7.52:1.5 | S: “Well then, reverends, come on the next sabbath day. | T: “Then in that case, my friends, come again on the next uposatha day,
an7.52:1.6 | S: Hopefully you’ll get to hear a Dhamma talk from the Buddha.” | T: and perhaps you’ll get to hear a Dhamma talk in the Blessed One’s presence.”
an7.52:1.7 | S: “Yes, sir” they replied. Then they rose from their seats, bowed to Sāriputta, and respectfully circled him before leaving. | T: “As you say, venerable sir,” the lay followers from Campā said to Ven. Sāriputta. Rising from their seats, bowing down to him, and then circling him—keeping him on their right—they left.
an7.52:2.1 | S: Then on the next sabbath the lay followers of Campā went to Venerable Sāriputta, bowed, and stood to one side. | T: Then, on the following uposatha day, the lay followers from Campā went to Ven. Sāriputta and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, stood to one side.
an7.52:2.2 | S: Then they went together with Sāriputta to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. Sāriputta said to the Buddha: | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta, together with the lay followers from Campā, went to the Blessed One and on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there, he said to the Blessed One:
an7.52:3.1 | S: “Sir, could it be that someone gives a gift and it is not very fruitful or beneficial, | T: “Might there be the case where a person gives a gift of a certain sort and it does not bear great fruit or great benefit, whereas another person gives a gift of the same sort and it bears great fruit and great benefit?”
an7.52:3.2 | S: while someone else gives exactly the same gift and it is very fruitful and beneficial?” | T: (none)
an7.52:3.3 | S: “Indeed it could, Sāriputta.” | T: “Yes, Sāriputta, there would be the case where a person gives a gift of a certain sort and it does not bear great fruit or great benefit, whereas another person gives a gift of the same sort and it bears great fruit and great benefit.”
an7.52:3.4 | P: siyā pana, sāriputta, idhekaccassa tādisaṁyeva dānaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalaṁ hoti mahānisaṁsan”ti. | T: (none)
an7.52:3.5 | S: “Sir, what is the cause, what is the reason for this?” | T: “Lord, what is the cause, what is the reason, why a person gives a gift of a certain sort and it does not bear great fruit or great benefit, whereas another person gives a gift of the same sort and it bears great fruit and great benefit?”
an7.52:3.6 | P: ko nu kho, bhante, hetu ko paccayo yena midhekaccassa tādisaṁyeva dānaṁ dinnaṁ mahapphalaṁ hoti mahānisaṁsan”ti? | T: (none)
an7.52:4.1 | S: “Sāriputta, take the case of a someone who gives a gift as an investment, their mind tied to it, expecting to keep it, thinking ‘I’ll enjoy this in my next life.’ | T: “Sāriputta, there is the case where a person gives a gift seeking his own profit, with a mind attached (to the reward), seeking to store up for himself (with the thought), ‘I’ll enjoy this after death.’
an7.52:4.2 | S: They give to ascetics or brahmins such things as food, drink, clothing, vehicles; garlands, fragrance, and makeup; and bed, house, and lighting. | T: He gives his gift—food, drink, clothing, a vehicle; a garland, perfume, & ointment; bedding, shelter, & a lamp—to a contemplative or a brahman.
an7.52:4.3 | S: What do you think, Sāriputta, don’t some people give gifts in this way?” | T: What do you think, Sāriputta? Might a person give such a gift as this?”
an7.52:4.4 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an7.52:5.1 | S: “Sāriputta, someone who gives a gift as an investment, | T: “Having given this gift seeking his own profit—with a mind attached (to the reward), seeking to store up for himself, (with the thought), ‘I’ll enjoy this after death’—
an7.52:5.2 | S: when their body breaks up, after death, is reborn in the company of the gods of the four great kings. | T: on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Four Great Kings.
an7.52:5.3 | S: When that deed, success, glory, and sovereignty is spent they return to this place. | T: Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:6.1 | S: Next, take the case of a someone who gives a gift not as an investment, their mind not tied to it, not expecting to keep it, and not thinking, ‘I’ll enjoy this in my next life.’ | T: “Then there is the case of a person who gives a gift not seeking his own profit, not with a mind attached (to the reward), not seeking to store up for himself, nor (with the thought), ‘I’ll enjoy this after death.’
an7.52:6.2 | S: But they give a gift thinking, ‘It’s good to give.’ … | T: Instead, he gives a gift with the thought, ‘Giving is good.’ He gives his gift—food, drink, clothing, a vehicle; a garland, perfume, & ointment; bedding, shelter, & a lamp—to a contemplative or a brahman. What do you think, Sāriputta? Might a person give such a gift as this?” “Yes, lord.” “Having given this gift with the thought, ‘Giving is good,’ on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Devas of the Thirty-three. Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:7.1 | P: Napi ‘sāhu dānan’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: (none)
an7.52:7.2 | S: They give a gift thinking, ‘Giving was practiced by my father and my father’s father. It would not be right for me to abandon this family custom.’ … | T: “Or, instead of thinking, ‘Giving is good,’ he gives a gift with the thought, ‘This was given in the past, done in the past, by my father & grandfather. It would not be right for me to let this old family custom be discontinued’… on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Devas of the Hours. Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:8.1 | P: Napi ‘dinnapubbaṁ katapubbaṁ pitupitāmahehi na arahāmi porāṇaṁ kulavaṁsaṁ hāpetun’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: (none)
an7.52:8.2 | S: They give a gift thinking, ‘I cook, they don’t. It wouldn’t be right for me to not give to them.’ … | T: “Or, instead… he gives a gift with the thought, ‘I am well-off. These are not well-off. It would not be right for me, being well-off, not to give a gift to those who are not well-off’ … on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Contented Devas. Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:9.1 | P: Napi ‘ahaṁ pacāmi, ime na pacanti, nārahāmi pacanto apacantānaṁ dānaṁ adātun’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: “Or, instead…
an7.52:9.2 | S: They give a gift thinking, ‘The ancient brahmin seers were Aṭṭhaka, Vāmaka, Vāmadeva, Vessāmitta, Yamadaggi, Aṅgīrasa, Bhāradvāja, Vāseṭṭha, Kassapa, and Bhagu. | T: he gives a gift with the thought, ‘Just as there were the great sacrifices of the sages of the past—Aṭṭhaka, Vāmaka, Vāmadeva, Vessāmitta, Yamadaggi, Aṇgīrasa, Bhāradvāja, Vāseṭṭha, Kassapa, & Bhagu—
an7.52:9.3 | S: Just as they performed great sacrifices, I will share a gift.’ … | T: in the same way will this be my distribution of gifts’ … on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Devas who Delight in Creation. Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:10.1 | P: Napi ‘yathā tesaṁ pubbakānaṁ isīnaṁ tāni mahāyaññāni ahesuṁ, seyyathidaṁ— | T: “Or, instead…
an7.52:10.2 | P: aṭṭhakassa vāmakassa vāmadevassa vessāmittassa yamadaggino aṅgīrasassa bhāradvājassa vāseṭṭhassa kassapassa bhaguno, evaṁ me ayaṁ dānasaṁvibhāgo bhavissatī’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: (none)
an7.52:10.3 | S: They give a gift thinking, ‘When giving this gift my mind becomes clear, and I become happy and joyful.’ … | T: he gives a gift with the thought, ‘When this gift of mine is given, it makes the mind serene. Gratification & joy arise’ … on the break-up of the body, after death, he reappears in the company of the Devas Wielding power over the creations of others. Then, having exhausted that action, that power, that status, that sovereignty, he is a returner, coming back to this world.
an7.52:11.1 | S: They don’t give a gift thinking, ‘When giving this gift my mind becomes clear, and I become happy and joyful.’ | T: “Or, instead of thinking, ‘When this gift of mine is given, it makes the mind serene. Gratification & joy arise,’
an7.52:11.2 | S: But they give a gift thinking, ‘This is an adornment and requisite for the mind.’ | T: he gives a gift with the thought, ‘This is an ornament for the mind, a support for the mind.’
an7.52:11.3 | S: They give to ascetics or brahmins such things as food, drink, clothing, vehicles; garlands, fragrance, and makeup; and bed, house, and lighting. | T: He gives his gift—food, drink, clothing, a vehicle; a garland, perfume, & ointment; bedding, shelter, & a lamp—to a contemplative or a brahman.
an7.52:11.4 | S: What do you think, Sāriputta, don’t some people give gifts in this way?” | T: What do you think, Sāriputta? Might a person give such a gift as this?”
an7.52:11.5 | S: “Yes, sir.” | T: “Yes, lord.”
an7.52:12.1 | S: “Sāriputta, someone who gives gifts, not for any other reason, | T: “Having given this, not seeking his own profit,
an7.52:12.2 | P: na patibaddhacitto dānaṁ deti; | T: not with a mind attached (to the reward),
an7.52:12.3 | P: na sannidhipekho dānaṁ deti; | T: not seeking to store up for himself,
an7.52:12.4 | P: na ‘imaṁ pecca paribhuñjissāmī’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: nor (with the thought), ‘I’ll enjoy this after death,’
an7.52:12.5 | P: napi ‘sāhu dānan’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: —nor with the thought, ‘Giving is good,’
an7.52:12.6 | P: napi ‘dinnapubbaṁ katapubbaṁ pitupitāmahehi na arahāmi porāṇaṁ kulavaṁsaṁ hāpetun’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: —nor with the thought, ‘This was given in the past, done in the past, by my father & grandfather. It would not be right for me to let this old family custom be discontinued,’
an7.52:12.7 | P: napi ‘ahaṁ pacāmi, ime na pacanti, nārahāmi pacanto apacantānaṁ dānaṁ adātun’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: —nor with the thought, ‘I am well-off. These are not well-off. It would not be right for me, being well-off, not to give a gift to those who are not well-off,’
an7.52:12.8 | P: napi ‘yathā tesaṁ pubbakānaṁ isīnaṁ tāni mahāyaññāni ahesuṁ, seyyathidaṁ— | T: —nor with the thought, ‘Just as there were the great sacrifices of the sages of the past—
an7.52:12.9 | P: aṭṭhakassa vāmakassa vāmadevassa vessāmittassa yamadaggino aṅgīrasassa bhāradvājassa vāseṭṭhassa kassapassa bhaguno, evaṁ me ayaṁ dānasaṁvibhāgo bhavissatī’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: Aṭṭhaka, Vāmaka, Vāmadeva, Vessāmitta, Yamadaggi, Aṇgīrasa, Bhāradvāja, Vāseṭṭha, Kassapa, & Bhagu—in the same way this will be my distribution of gifts,’
an7.52:12.10 | P: napi ‘imaṁ me dānaṁ dadato cittaṁ pasīdati, attamanatā somanassaṁ upajāyatī’ti dānaṁ deti; | T: —nor with the thought, ‘When this gift of mine is given, it makes the mind serene. Gratification & joy arise,’
an7.52:12.11 | S: but thinking, ‘This is an adornment and requisite for the mind’, | T: —but with the thought, ‘This is an ornament for the mind, a support for the mind’—

