## sn35.117
sn35.117:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, before my awakening—when I was still unawakened but intent on awakening—I thought: | T: “Monks, before my awakening, when I was still just an unawakened bodhisatta, the thought occurred to me:
sn35.117:1.2 | S: ‘My mind might often stray toward the five kinds of sensual stimulation that I formerly experienced—which have passed, ceased, and perished—or to those in the present, or in the future a little.’ | T: ‘Those five strings of sensuality that previously made contact with my awareness—that are past, ceased, changed: My mind, having often gone there, might go to those that are present, or occasionally to those that are future.’
sn35.117:1.3 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: Then the thought occurred to me:
sn35.117:1.4 | S: ‘In my own way I should practice diligence, mindfulness, and protecting the mind regarding the five kinds of sensual stimulation that I formerly experienced—which have passed, ceased, and perished.’ | T: ‘Those five strings of sensuality that previously made contact with my awareness—that are past, ceased, changed: There, for my own sake, heedfulness, mindfulness, and a protection of my awareness should be practiced.
sn35.117:1.5 | S: So, mendicants, your minds might also often stray toward the five kinds of sensual stimulation that you formerly experienced—which have passed, ceased, and perished—or to those in the present, or in the future a little. | T: “Therefore, monks, those five strings of sensuality that previously made contact with your awareness, too—that are past, ceased, changed: Your mind, having often gone there, might go to those that are present, or occasionally to those that are future.
sn35.117:1.6 | S: So in your own way you should practice diligence, mindfulness, and protecting the mind regarding the five kinds of sensual stimulation that you formerly experienced—which have passed, ceased, and perished. | T: Therefore, those five strings of sensuality that previously made contact with your awareness, too—that are past, ceased, changed: There, for your own sake, heedfulness, mindfulness, and a protection of your awareness should be practiced.
sn35.117:1.7 | S: So you should understand that dimension where the eye ceases and perception of sights fades away. | T: “Therefore, monks, that dimension should be experienced where the eye [vision] ceases and the perception [mental label] of form fades.
sn35.117:1.8 | S: You should understand that dimension where the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: That dimension should be experienced where the ear ceases and the perception of sound fades. That dimension should be experienced where the nose ceases and the perception of aroma fades. That dimension should be experienced where the tongue ceases and the perception of flavor fades. That dimension should be experienced where the body ceases and the perception of tactile sensation fades.
sn35.117:1.9 | S: mind ceases and perception of ideas fades away.” | T: That dimension should be experienced where the intellect ceases and the perception of idea fades. That dimension should be experienced.”
sn35.117:1.10 | S: When he had spoken, the Blessed One rose from his seat and entered his dwelling. | T: Having said this, the Blessed One got up from his seat and went into his dwelling.
sn35.117:2.1 | S: Soon after the Buddha left, those mendicants considered, | T: Then, not long after the Blessed One had left, this thought occurred to the monks:
