sn35.117:5.11 | P: Tesaṁ no, āvuso, amhākaṁ etadahosi: | T: (none)
sn35.117:5.12 | P: ‘ayaṁ kho āyasmā ānando satthu ceva saṁvaṇṇito, sambhāvito ca viññūnaṁ sabrahmacārīnaṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.117:5.13 | P: Pahoti cāyasmā ānando imassa bhagavatā saṅkhittena uddesassa uddiṭṭhassa vitthārena atthaṁ avibhattassa vitthārena atthaṁ vibhajituṁ. | T: (none)
sn35.117:5.14 | P: Yannūna mayaṁ yenāyasmā ānando tenupasaṅkameyyāma; upasaṅkamitvā āyasmantaṁ ānandaṁ etamatthaṁ paṭipuccheyyāmā’ti. | T: (none)
sn35.117:5.15 | S: “May Venerable Ānanda please explain this.” | T: “Analyze the meaning, Ven. Ānanda!”
sn35.117:6.1 | S: “Reverends, suppose there was a person in need of heartwood. …” | T: [He replied:] “Friends, it’s as if a man needing heartwood, looking for heartwood, wandering in search of heartwood—passing over the root & trunk of a standing tree possessing heartwood—were to imagine that heartwood should be sought among its branches & leaves. So it is with you, who—having bypassed the Blessed One when you were face to face with him, the Teacher—imagine that I should be asked about this matter. For knowing, the Blessed One knows; seeing, he sees. He is the Eye, he is Knowledge, he is Dhamma, he is Brahmā. He is the speaker, the proclaimer, the elucidator of meaning, the giver of the deathless, the lord of the Dhamma, the Tathāgata. That was the time when you should have cross-questioned him about this matter. However he answered, that was how you should have remembered it.”
sn35.117:6.2 | S: “Please explain this, if it’s no trouble.” | T: “Yes, friend Ānanda: Knowing, the Blessed One knows; seeing, he sees. He is the Eye, he is Knowledge, he is Dhamma, he is Brahmā. He is the speaker, the proclaimer, the elucidator of meaning, the giver of the deathless, the lord of the Dhamma, the Tathāgata. That was the time when we should have cross-questioned him about this matter. However he answered, that was how we should have remembered it. But you are praised by the Teacher and esteemed by your observant companions in the holy life. You are capable of analyzing the unanalyzed detailed meaning of this brief statement. Analyze the meaning, Ven. Ānanda without making it difficult!”
sn35.117:7.1 | S: “Then listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “In that case, my friends, listen & pay close attention. I will speak.”
sn35.117:7.2 | S: “Yes, reverend,” they replied. | T: “As you say, friend,” the monks responded to him.
sn35.117:7.3 | S: Ānanda said this: | T: Ven. Ānanda said this:
sn35.117:8.1 | S: “Reverends, the Buddha gave this brief summary, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail: | T: “Friends, concerning the brief statement the Blessed One made, after which he went into his dwelling without analyzing the detailed meaning—i.e.,
sn35.117:8.2 | S: ‘So you should understand that dimension where the eye ceases and perception of sights fades away. You should understand that dimension where the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: ‘Therefore, monks, that dimension should be experienced where the eye ceases and the perception of form fades. That dimension should be experienced where the ear ceases and the perception of sound fades… where the nose ceases and the perception of aroma fades… where the tongue ceases and the perception of flavor fades… where the body ceases and the perception of tactile sensation fades…
sn35.117:8.3 | S: mind ceases and perception of ideas fades away.’ | T: where the intellect ceases and the perception of idea fades. That dimension should be experienced’—
sn35.117:8.4 | S: And this is how I understand the detailed meaning of this summary. | T: I understand the detailed meaning to be this:
sn35.117:8.5 | S: The Buddha was referring to the cessation of the six sense fields when he said: | T: This was stated by the Blessed One with regard to the cessation of the six sense media.”
sn35.117:8.6 | S: ‘So you should understand that dimension where the eye ceases and perception of sights fades away. You should understand that dimension where the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “So, concerning the brief statement the Blessed One made, after which he entered his dwelling without analyzing the detailed meaning—i.e., ‘Therefore, monks, that dimension should be experienced where the eye ceases and the perception of form fades. That dimension should be experienced where the ear ceases and the perception of sound fades… where the nose ceases and the perception of aroma fades… where the tongue ceases and the perception of flavor fades… where the body ceases and the perception of tactile sensation fades…
sn35.117:8.7 | S: mind ceases and perception of ideas fades away.’ | T: where the intellect ceases and the perception of idea fades. That dimension should be experienced’—
sn35.117:8.8 | S: The Buddha gave this brief summary, then entered his dwelling without explaining the meaning in detail. | T: (none)
sn35.117:8.9 | P: ‘tasmātiha, bhikkhave, se āyatane veditabbe yattha cakkhu ca nirujjhati, rūpasaññā ca nirujjhati, se āyatane veditabbe …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.117:8.10 | P: yattha mano ca nirujjhati, dhammasaññā ca nirujjhati, se āyatane veditabbe’ti. | T: (none)
sn35.117:8.11 | S: And this is how I understand the detailed meaning of this summary. | T: this is how I understand the detailed meaning.
sn35.117:8.12 | S: If you wish, you may go to the Buddha and ask him about this. | T: Now, friends, if you wish, having gone to the Blessed One, cross-question him about this matter.
sn35.117:8.13 | P: upasaṅkamitvā etamatthaṁ puccheyyātha. | T: (none)
sn35.117:8.14 | S: You should remember it in line with the Buddha’s answer.” | T: However he answers is how you should remember it.”
sn35.117:9.1 | S: “Yes, reverend,” replied those mendicants. Then they rose from their seats and went to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. | T: Then the monks, delighting in & approving of Ven. Ānanda’s words, got up from their seats and went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to him, they sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they (told him what had happened after he had gone into his dwelling,
